Reviewing ex00, why is #include <unistd.h>
  # : it's for C preprocessor, not a common C statement.
    file .c -> preprocessor (using #...) -> compiler (to recode C source codes) -> codes can be executed
  include : include the contents/information from the following header file
    quote the statements offered by the header unistd.h
  <> : it's a header offered by the system, please find it in system's header
        we can create our own header, local headers: "..."
        system headers: <...>
  unistd : UNIX standard
  .h : header file
    other examples:
      .c = C source file

